  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 207 AM EDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nA frontal boundary and ~2 inch PWAT axis will hover in west to \neast fashion over the area through at least Thursday with \nperiodic shower/storm chances. Locally heavy rainfall/flash \nflooding will be the primary concern given the high moisture \ncontent and potential for several rounds of slow moving \nconvection. \n\nFavorable right entrance region upper support, in conjunction \nwith an incoming convectively augmented mid level impulse \n(potential MCV), should keep at least scattered convection in \nthe vicinity of the theta-e ridge this morning, especially \nacross northern IN and northwest OH. Rainfall rates have been \nimpressive (over an inch in 10-15 minutes), so will continue to \nmonitor flooding going forward.\n\nWeak height rises on the northern fringes of a stout upper level \nridge suggest some overall subsidence this afternoon into this \nevening. Held with lower PoPs as a result during this time, but \nstill cannot rule out widely scattered convection within any pockets \nof greater heating, mainly near the US 24 corridor. Later tonight \ninto Thursday morning then features increasing chances for \nanother round of storms and potential hydro issues as renewed \nwarm advection ensues within another low level jet. \n\nThe bulk of available model guidance does push the effective frontal \nzone off to south-southwest by Thursday night into Friday with brief \ndry and less humid conditions. Rain/storm chances then return \nthis weekend as piecemeal shortwave energy ejects east out of \nthe Intermountain West, forcing the boundary and instability \naxis back north into the lower Great Lakes.",
